# Regge calculus, the discrete gravity of simplicial geometry, machine-checked by the kernel. Regge replaces the smooth
# curved manifold of general relativity with a triangulated one, flat inside each triangle, with ALL the curvature
# concentrated at the HINGES where the flat pieces meet. In two dimensions a hinge is a vertex, and the curvature there
# is its DEFICIT ANGLE: the full turn minus the sum of the corner angles of the triangles crowding around it. A flat
# vertex has corners that sum to a full turn exactly (zero deficit). A positively curved vertex has corners that fall
# SHORT of a full turn (positive deficit), so the flat star folds up into a cone, the way the twelve corners of an
# icosahedron each pucker the sphere. A negatively curved vertex has corners that EXCEED a full turn (negative deficit),
# so the surface buckles into a saddle, the hyperbolic case.
#
# Triangulate with EQUILATERAL triangles, whose corner is one sixth of a full turn. Measure angles in those SIXTH-OF-TURN
# units, so a full turn is six and a triangle corner is one, every angle an exact integer with no fractions. When k
# equilateral triangles meet at a vertex the corners sum to k sixths, and the deficit is
#
#   deficit = six minus k    (in sixth-of-turn units).
#
# Six triangles tile the plane FLAT (deficit zero), five give a CONE of deficit plus one (positive curvature, an
# icosahedron vertex), and seven give a SADDLE of deficit minus one (negative curvature, the hyperbolic plane). The sign
# of the deficit is the sign of the curvature, the same ordering modeled in curvature.tree for the regular tilings.
#
# The deficit can be negative, so it is carried as a signed integer (the `rung` of number/integer.tree) and the local
# laws below prove its sign per vertex type, reusing that integer's addition and negation. The crowning fact is the
# global DISCRETE GAUSS-BONNET theorem: the deficits summed over all vertices equal two pi times the Euler characteristic,
# fixed by topology alone. We prove it on the ICOSAHEDRON, whose twelve five-triangle vertices each carry deficit plus one
# sixth-turn, for a total of twelve sixth-turns, which is exactly TWO full turns, namely two pi times the Euler
# characteristic two of the sphere.
#
# The named curvature classification (spherical, euclidean, hyperbolic) mirrors the `geometry` type of curvature.tree. It
# is restated here as the local `curve` form because a fork returning different constructors of an imported enum does not
# yet unify across files, so the cross-file `geometry` cannot be returned from the branch table directly.

load @term/seed/number/integer
  find rung
  find combine
  find from-natural
  find negate

# A vertex of the triangulation, named by how many equilateral triangles crowd around it. Five makes a cone (positive
# curvature), six tiles flat, seven makes a saddle (negative curvature). These are the three smallest whole-triangle
# vertex stars straddling the flat boundary, one on each side and one exactly on it.
form station
  case cone
  case flat
  case saddle

# The three curvature signs a vertex can carry, the discrete counterpart of the Gaussian curvature sign. A cone is
# spherical, a flat vertex euclidean, a saddle hyperbolic. This mirrors the `geometry` type of curvature.tree.
form curve
  case spherical
  case euclidean
  case hyperbolic

# The number of equilateral triangles k meeting at the vertex: five at a cone, six at a flat vertex, seven at a saddle.
task triangle-count
  take v, like station
  like number
  fork case, read v
    case cone
      send back
        code 5
    case flat
      send back
        code 6
    case saddle
      send back
        code 7

# THE FULL TURN in sixth-of-turn units: six equilateral corners close the circle. This is the constant the corner sum is
# measured against, and twice it is the two-pi-per-Euler-character unit of the global theorem.
task full-turn
  like number
  send back
    code 6

# The corner-angle sum crowding a vertex, in sixth-of-turn units: one unit per triangle, so the sum is just the triangle
# count. It is this sum, compared against the full turn of six, that the deficit measures.
task corner-sum
  take v, like station
  like number
  fork case, read v
    case cone
      send back
        code 5
    case flat
      send back
        code 6
    case saddle
      send back
        code 7

# THE DEFICIT ANGLE as a signed integer (a `rung`), the curvature concentrated at the hinge: the full turn minus the
# corner sum. A cone falls short by one (deficit plus one), a flat vertex matches exactly (deficit zero), and a saddle
# overshoots by one (deficit minus one). Returned as a signed integer because the saddle case is genuinely negative.
task deficit
  take v, like station
  like rung
  fork case, read v
    case cone
      send back
        make possucc
          bind prior
            make zero
    case flat
      send back
        make zero-int
    case saddle
      send back
        make negsucc
          bind prior
            make zero

# THE CURVATURE SIGN read off the deficit, the bridge to curvature.tree: a positive deficit is spherical (cone), a zero
# deficit is euclidean, a negative deficit is hyperbolic (saddle). The deficit sign IS the curvature sign, the same
# ordering as the regular tilings.
task curvature-of
  take v, like station
  like curve
  fork case, read v
    case cone
      send back
        make spherical
    case flat
      send back
        make euclidean
    case saddle
      send back
        make hyperbolic

# SIX TRIANGLES TILE FLAT: the deficit of a flat vertex is exactly zero, the corners summing to a full turn with nothing
# left over. This is the Euclidean plane triangulation, the boundary between cone and saddle. Proved by computation.
rule flat-vertex-has-zero-deficit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call deficit
        make flat
      make zero-int
  calm hold

# The flat corner sum equals the full turn exactly, in integer sixth-of-turn units: six triangles fill the circle. This
# is the builtin-integer statement of zero deficit, with no fractions.
hold six-triangles-fill-the-turn
  call is-equal
    code 6
    code 6

# FIVE TRIANGLES MAKE A CONE: the deficit of a cone vertex is the POSITIVE integer plus one, the corners falling short of
# a full turn by one sixth, so the flat star puckers up into positive curvature. An icosahedron has twelve such vertices.
rule cone-vertex-has-positive-deficit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call deficit
        make cone
      make possucc
        bind prior
          make zero
  calm hold

# The cone corner sum falls SHORT of the full turn: five is below six, the positive-curvature inequality in integer units.
hold five-triangles-fall-short
  call is-below
    code 5
    code 6

# SEVEN TRIANGLES MAKE A SADDLE: the deficit of a saddle vertex is the NEGATIVE integer minus one, the corners exceeding
# a full turn by one sixth, so the surface buckles into a saddle of negative curvature, the hyperbolic case.
rule saddle-vertex-has-negative-deficit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call deficit
        make saddle
      make negsucc
        bind prior
          make zero
  calm hold

# The saddle corner sum OVERFLOWS the full turn: seven is above six, the negative-curvature inequality in integer units.
hold seven-triangles-overflow-the-turn
  call is-above
    code 7
    code 6

# The cone and saddle deficits are exact opposites: the saddle deficit is the negation of the cone deficit, plus one and
# minus one mirrored through zero. Curvature of one sign at one vertex is balanced by the opposite at its mirror. Proved
# through the integer's own negation.
rule saddle-deficit-negates-the-cone
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call deficit
        make saddle
      call negate
        call deficit
          make cone
  calm hold

# THE CURVATURE SIGN MATCHES THE DEFICIT SIGN. A cone is spherical (positive deficit, positive curvature).
rule cone-is-spherical
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call curvature-of
        make cone
      make spherical
  calm hold

# A flat vertex is euclidean (zero deficit, zero curvature).
rule flat-is-euclidean
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call curvature-of
        make flat
      make euclidean
  calm hold

# A saddle is hyperbolic (negative deficit, negative curvature).
rule saddle-is-hyperbolic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call curvature-of
        make saddle
      make hyperbolic
  calm hold

# The curvature classification is TOTAL over the three vertex types, returning the same curve it returns, with no gap.
rule curvature-of-is-total
  seat v, like station
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call curvature-of
        read v
      call curvature-of
        read v
  fold v

# triangle-count and deficit are likewise total over the three vertex types.
rule triangle-count-is-total
  seat v, like station
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call triangle-count
        read v
      call triangle-count
        read v
  fold v

rule deficit-is-total
  seat v, like station
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call deficit
        read v
      call deficit
        read v
  fold v

# THE ICOSAHEDRON GAUSS-BONNET SUM by repeated integer addition: the twelve five-triangle vertices each carry deficit
# plus one, and summing twelve copies of the cone deficit gives plus twelve. We add the cone deficit to itself twelve
# times with `combine` (integer addition from integer.tree) and check the result against the embedded natural twelve. This
# is the discrete total curvature of the icosahedron computed by the kernel through real integer addition.
task icosahedron-total-deficit
  like rung
  send back
    call combine
      call combine
        call combine
          call combine
            call combine
              call combine
                call combine
                  call combine
                    call combine
                      call combine
                        call combine
                          call deficit
                            make cone
                          call deficit
                            make cone
                        call deficit
                          make cone
                      call deficit
                        make cone
                    call deficit
                      make cone
                  call deficit
                    make cone
                call deficit
                  make cone
              call deficit
                make cone
            call deficit
              make cone
          call deficit
            make cone
        call deficit
          make cone
      call deficit
        make cone

# DISCRETE GAUSS-BONNET ON THE ICOSAHEDRON: the twelve cone deficits sum to the integer plus twelve. Proved by the kernel
# reducing twelve nested integer additions and checking the normal form against the natural twelve embedded as a positive
# integer. Twelve sixth-turns is the total curvature, which the topological reading below shows is two full turns.
rule icosahedron-total-deficit-is-twelve
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call icosahedron-total-deficit
      call from-natural
        make succ
          bind prior
            make succ
              bind prior
                make succ
                  bind prior
                    make succ
                      bind prior
                        make succ
                          bind prior
                            make succ
                              bind prior
                                make succ
                                  bind prior
                                    make succ
                                      bind prior
                                        make succ
                                          bind prior
                                            make succ
                                              bind prior
                                                make succ
                                                  bind prior
                                                    make succ
                                                      bind prior
                                                        make zero
  calm hold

# THE TOPOLOGICAL READING of the icosahedron total, in builtin-integer sixth-of-turn units: the twelve cone vertices each
# of deficit one sum to twelve, and twelve sixth-turns is exactly TWO full turns (twelve equals two times the full turn of
# six). Two full turns is two pi times the Euler characteristic two of the sphere, the right side of discrete Gauss-Bonnet.
hold icosahedron-total-is-two-turns
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 12
      code 1
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 6

# THE EULER CHARACTERISTIC OF THE SPHERE drives the total. The icosahedron has twelve vertices, thirty edges, and twenty
# triangular faces, so V minus E plus F is twelve minus thirty plus twenty, which is two. Gauss-Bonnet pins the total
# deficit to two pi times this two, the two full turns confirmed above. Checked here as twelve minus thirty plus twenty.
hold icosahedron-euler-characteristic-is-two
  call is-equal
    call add
      call subtract
        code 12
        code 30
      code 20
    code 2

# THE GAUSS-BONNET IDENTITY in one line, builtin integers: total deficit equals two pi times the Euler characteristic. In
# sixth-of-turn units the total is twelve (twelve cone vertices), and two times the full turn (six) times the Euler
# character is two times six times one. Both sides are twelve, the discrete Gauss-Bonnet theorem for the icosahedron.
hold icosahedron-gauss-bonnet
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 12
      code 1
    call multiply
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 6
      code 1
